    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d400">

                <pb facs="0476" n="358" xml:id="pg358"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00Sonora/236: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul at Chihuahua</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Myers</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-03-11T18:00:00-05:00">March 11, 1929—6
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your March 8, 6 p.m.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d400-f1"/> is not clear to the
                    Department. What do you mean by General Moseley’s “warning” to the Governor of
                    Chihuahua and on whom did it make “a very bad impression”?</p>
                <p>See Department’s circular of March 8, 8 p.m.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d400-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d400-f1" n="11" xml:id="d400-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram of March 9, 11 a.m., from the Consul at Nogales, <ref
                        target="#pg354">p. 354</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d400-f2" n="12" xml:id="d400-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 81, March 8, midnight, to the Ambassador in Mexico, <ref
                        target="#pg354">p. 354</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
